Connect pH with activity, the water model, and a calibrated observation (@gc-ph: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @general-chemistry, #source "../sources/IUPAC-Green-Book.lingua#r_5SR8CP04E580VSV69MYQNSZ73F", #source "../sources/Chem1-Lower.lingua#r_0NFQA43WZVMECZY6SKZJMRXWBZ", #source "../../../../inquiry/metrology/sources/GUM-Examples-2026.lingua#r_4FVN1GYTH9X8M1JFWFNT696GR0", #general-chemistry "Weak-acids-and-valid-approximations.lingua#r_1X16MN6WMFKKBA6XGFSSHZB8XP") { r_3HDBH538NRM4TYDQ5SJVM04NRE
**Read:** [Quantities, Units and Symbols in Physical Chemistry — IUPAC Green Book](../sources/IUPAC-Green-Book.lingua#r_5SR8CP04E580VSV69MYQNSZ73F), hydrogen-ion activity and related quantity conventions; [Chem1 General Chemistry Virtual Textbook — Stephen Lower](../sources/Chem1-Lower.lingua#r_0NFQA43WZVMECZY6SKZJMRXWBZ), pH and the advanced aqueous acid/base treatment; [Worked measurement examples — JCGM GUM-5:2026](../../../../inquiry/metrology/sources/GUM-Examples-2026.lingua#r_4FVN1GYTH9X8M1JFWFNT696GR0), the complete published pH example and its measurement assumptions.

pH is defined through hydrogen-ion activity under its adopted conventions. A simple ideal concentration approximation uses pH ≈ −log₁₀([H⁺]/c°), with c° = 1 mol L⁻¹. Keep the standard quantity explicit so the logarithm's argument is dimensionless. High precision requires more than counting decimal places in a concentration calculation.

The water-ion relation and temperature determine a neutral point within a chosen model. The familiar value near seven refers to particular aqueous conditions, not a universal neutral pH at every temperature and medium. A measured pH comes from a procedure, calibration, reference buffers, response, and conventions; it is not a direct independent count of single-ion activity without that framework.

A glass-electrode reading can depend on temperature, liquid-junction effects, calibration range, matrix, equilibration, and handling. Distinguish the chemical speciation model from the instrument's observation model. Use the shared metrology and published pH example for a deeper measurement account.

**Work:** convert a set of explicitly ideal concentrations into pH values and invert them, checking the reference concentration. Analyze an extremely dilute acid where water autoprotolysis cannot be ignored. Read a complete pH-measurement example and identify what its reported uncertainty covers and which sample conditions matter.

Read the Green Book definitions and the advanced Chem1 discussion, comparing them with the first textbook treatment. Preserve both the easy computational entrance and the later operational/conventional limits. For an actual laboratory study report method, temperature, calibration, matrix, uncertainty, and interpretation together.

**Continue:** [Derive a weak-acid calculation and test its approximation](Weak-acids-and-valid-approximations.lingua#r_1X16MN6WMFKKBA6XGFSSHZB8XP).

